00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.905
YUFENG GUO:
TensorFlow.js is awesome.

00:00:03.905 --> 00:00:05.960
Keras is awesome.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.850
Wouldn't it be extra amazing
if they could work together?

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:11.530
Well, I got great news for you.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:12.640
They do!

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.059
Stay tuned to find out just how
to import your trained Keras

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:18.140
model into TensorFlow.js.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:30.550
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.470
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:35.200
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.890
we're going to look at what
it takes to import a Keras

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.660
model into TensorFlow.js.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.250
TensorFlow.js has
many functionalities,

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.820
but perhaps one of
the most exciting ones

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.600
is its ability to operate in
collaboration with other tools.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.280
We're really seeing
a future where

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.240
you can do machine learning
across a range of platforms,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.690
languages, and
devices, all optimized

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.060
to perform at their
peak performance

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.540
in the situation they're
most well-suited for.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.130
The tricky thing about loading
a Keras model into TensorFlow.js

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:16.110
is that Keras models are
typically saved as HDF5 files,

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:20.880
using something like
model.save('my_model.h5').

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.800
TensorFlow.js provides a tool,
the TensorFlow.js converter,

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.920
which is used to convert your
Keras model into a format

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.320
that TensorFlow.js
can consume, which

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.160
is a folder containing a
file called model.json.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.830
Let's see that in context
with the Keras model

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.950
that we've been using in
the last few episodes.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.020
In the episode where we
converted a Keras model

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:46.350
to a TensorFlow estimator, we
also exported our Keras model

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.430
as a .h5 file, so we'll
head on over to that Kernel

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.970
and download the file
from the Output section.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:57.300
So I've got my Keras model
downloaded and named it

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.350
keras_model.h5 just to keep
things clear which file is from

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:02.370
which library.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:06.630
Now I need to run pip install
tensorflowjs in my Python

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.389
environment to
install the converter.

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:13.580
Notice that the pip library
name is tensorflowjs,

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.320
even though the converter
we'll use is called

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.960
tensorflowjs converter.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.870
OK, so we've got the converter
and we got the Keras model

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.580
all in one place.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.130
One last step before we
run the converter, though.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.080
I'm going to create a
new folder because we

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.470
need to store the outputs of
the TensorFlow.js model files.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:34.980
Let's just call it tfjs_files.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.200
Now we can run the
converter, passing in a flag

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.600
to indicate that it
is a Keras model,

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.640
and we'll also need to pass
the path to the Keras HDF5 file

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.430
in that folder we just created
so it has a destination

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.470
to put the new files in.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.630
Once our converter
has run, let's

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.520
take a peek inside the folder.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.510
We see that it's got not only
the model.json file, but three

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.810
additional files.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:02.740
There's a group1-shard1of1,
group2-shard1of1, and

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.250
group3-shard1of1.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:09.090
These shards are here to
enable faster repeat loading

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.710
by the browser.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.950
The files are below the
typical cache size limit,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:16.710
so they'll likely be
cached for subsequent calls

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:19.200
when you're serving them.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.500
So after you've successfully
converted your Keras model

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.940
to model.json, you've just
got one last step left--

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:28.140
got to load it into
your JavaScript.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.140
Thankfully, this
is a one-line task.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:35.820
Go ahead and use tf.modelload
to point to the URL

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.930
where you have the files
posted, and TensorFlow.js

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.390
takes care of the rest.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.210
It's worth pointing out
here that since the model is

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:49.060
operating on the client
side, using JSON files,

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:53.430
it's not a great use case if
you need to secure your model.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:57.660
For that, you'll have to
stick with client side models.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:58.260
All right.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.510
So now you're ready to
import your own Keras

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:04.260
models into TensorFlow.js.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.140
Go forth and make the
next great machine

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.480
learning-inspired
game on the web.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.660
Thanks for watching this
episode of "Cloud AI

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.630
Adventures," and if you
